    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor initially blocks the current as it charges, but once fully charged, it acts like an open circuit. Conversely, in an AC circuit, the capacitor constantly charges and discharges, allowing a current to flow, though with a phase shift from the voltage. The formula for capacitive reactance ($\\text{X}_\\text{C}$) is $\\text{X}_\\text{C} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\text{f}\\text{C}}$, where $\\text{f}$ is the frequency and $\\text{C}$ is the capacitance.",
