    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so in the ac one, is the current basically just constantly moving through because of the frequency? could u explain why the reactance goes down when the frequency goes up? i'm having a hard time visualizing why that happens.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, in AC, the continuous change in voltage forces the capacitor to continually charge and discharge, resulting in a constantly moving current. Capacitive reactance decreases with increasing frequency because the capacitor has less time to build up its charge to block the changing current. At higher frequencies, the rate of change is so fast that the capacitor acts more like a short circuit, allowing more current to pass through it.",
